(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-12-07"] = {"7.1":{"s":"A linear inequation in two variables, such as ax + by ≤ c, is shown on the XY plane by shading one side of the line ax + by = c: a half plane. To pick the side, test a point such as the origin: if it satisfies the inequality, shade its side. The common shaded region of several inequalities is their solution set. A convex set is one where the segment joining any two of its points stays inside.","w":["Test the origin only if the line does not pass through it; otherwise test another point.","x ≥ 0 and y ≥ 0 limit the region to the first quadrant."],"x":["A linear inequation in two variables has the form ax + by ≤ c (or ≥, less than, greater than). Its graph is a half plane: the line ax + by = c divides the plane into two parts, and the points of one part satisfy the inequation. The line itself is included when the sign is ≤ or ≥.","How to draw the region: (1) draw the line ax + by = c, using its intercepts on the two axes (put y = 0 to find x, and x = 0 to find y); (2) pick a test point that is not on the line, for example the origin; (3) put it into the inequation. If it is true, shade the side containing the point. If it is false, shade the other side.","If the line passes through the origin, test another point, such as (1, 0) or (0, 1). The conditions x ≥ 0 and y ≥ 0 limit the region to the first quadrant, and are part of every problem here.","A system of inequations is solved by drawing all the half planes and taking the part common to all of them. This is the solution set. A set is convex if the segment between any two of its points stays inside the set. The region of a system of linear inequations is always convex. Its boundary points where two lines meet are the corner points."],"e":"Draw x + y ≤ 4 in the first quadrant. The line meets the axes at (4, 0) and (0, 4). At the origin, 0 ≤ 4 is true, so shade the side with the origin. The solution is the triangle with vertices (0, 0), (4, 0), (0, 4)."},"7.2":{"s":"A linear programming problem asks for the largest or smallest value of a linear objective function, such as a profit or a cost, subject to linear constraints and x, y ≥ 0. Formulate it from the word problem, draw the feasible region, and find its corner points. When the feasible region is bounded, the best value of the objective function is reached at a corner point: compute it at each corner and compare.","w":["Name the unknowns first, then write the objective function and each constraint from the table of data.","For a bounded feasible region, the maximum and minimum are found among its corner points."],"x":["A linear programming problem (LPP) asks for the largest or the smallest value of a linear function, called the objective function, such as profit or cost. The variables must satisfy a set of linear inequations, called constraints, and x ≥ 0, y ≥ 0. The region of points that satisfy all the constraints is the feasible region. Its points are feasible solutions, and the best one is the optimal solution.","Formulating a problem from words: (1) name the unknown quantities as x and y; (2) write the objective function, for example Z = 20x + 30y for a profit of ₹20 per unit of A and ₹30 per unit of B; (3) write one constraint for each resource, using a table of data. A limit such as 'at most 40 hours' gives ≤, and a requirement such as 'at least 8 units' gives ≥.","The corner point method: (1) draw the feasible region; (2) find the corner points, by solving the equations of each pair of boundary lines that meet there; (3) compute Z at each corner point; (4) the largest value is the maximum, and the smallest is the minimum. For a bounded region, the maximum and the minimum are always at corner points.","Special cases. If two corner points give the same best value, every point on the segment between them does too, so there are many optimal solutions. If the region is unbounded, a maximum may not exist. For a minimum with positive coefficients over an unbounded region in the first quadrant, a minimum exists at a corner point. If there are no common points, the problem is infeasible.","Common mistakes: reversing a sign in a constraint, forgetting x ≥ 0 and y ≥ 0, and checking only some of the corner points."],"e":"Maximise Z = 3x + 2y subject to x + y ≤ 4, x ≤ 3, x, y ≥ 0. The corner points are (0, 0), (3, 0), (3, 1), (0, 4). The values of Z are 0, 9, 11 and 8. The maximum is 11 at (3, 1)."}};
